WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.814
Perseverance has driven farther on Mars than any rover before it, thanks to its autonav hazard system. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 223. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, now credits Perseverance with the longest distance traveled on another world, meaning Mars and Earth's moon. Universe today notes that more distance traveled means more scientific objectives are reached. Autonav lets the rover analyze terrain and choose safe paths without waiting for commands from Earth.

00:00:29.814 --> 00:00:34.934
Each successful segment adds to the total while delivering new images and rock samples

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:35.534
for study.

00:00:36.114 --> 00:00:40.994
The system processes hazard data in real time, turning what would have been long pauses

00:00:40.994 --> 00:00:42.494
into steady forward motion.

00:00:43.114 --> 00:00:47.714
That onboard decision-making allows continuous progress across bolder strewn planes.

00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:53.729
NASA will preview two upcoming spacewalks to complete solar array installation and power

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:57.149
augmentation of the International Space Station, or ISS.

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:03.309
A news conference is set for 2 p.m. Eastern daylight time on Tuesday, October 20th, from

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:08.029
NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston, and NASA will stream the event through a variety

00:01:08.029 --> 00:01:08.829
of platforms.

00:01:09.309 --> 00:01:15.489
During U.S. Spacewalk 100, NASA astronaut Anil Menon and Canadian Space Agency astronaut

00:01:15.489 --> 00:01:18.889
Joshua Kutrick will exit the station's Quest Airlock.

00:01:19.389 --> 00:01:24.909
They will install and deploy an International Space Station Rollout Solar Array or Irosa

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:27.229
on the 2A electrical power channel.

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:31.929
During US Spacewalk 101, Menon and Kutrick will install

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:34.249
and deploy the eighth and final Irosa

00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:36.449
on the 3B channel of the space station,

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:38.949
completing the power augmentation upgrades.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:42.569
Together, the eight rollout arrays will increase available power

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:45.949
by up to 30%, boosting the station's electric power

00:01:45.949 --> 00:01:48.949
generation capacity from 160 kilowatts

00:01:48.949 --> 00:01:51.489
to approximately 215 kilowatts.

00:01:51.489 --> 00:01:58.049
The spacewalks will be the 285th and 286th conducted in support of space station assembly,

00:01:58.609 --> 00:01:59.629
maintenance and upgrades.

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:05.587
Astronomers have used NASA's Chandra X-ray Observatory to break down the cause of a remarkable

00:02:05.587 --> 00:02:08.607
formation of radio waves nicknamed the treble clef.

00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:14.467
A paper describing the findings led by Andrea Baution of the National Institute for Astrophysics

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:18.467
in Italy published Wednesday in the Astronomy and Astrophysics Journal.

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:24.267
Chandra and Erozita found diffuse hot gas at the location of the treble clef, showing

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:26.467
that a galaxy cluster hosts the object.

00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:31.567
The researchers also determined the galaxy cluster is in fact two clusters in the process

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:32.127
of merging.

00:02:32.887 --> 00:02:38.007
Radio data from low-far revealed the musical shape after an earlier black hole outburst drove

00:02:38.007 --> 00:02:43.307
charged particles outward at close to the speed of light, and the later collision re-energized

00:02:43.307 --> 00:02:43.787
the cloud.

00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:48.727
The authors concluded that the treble cleft likely formed in a two-stage process.

00:02:49.287 --> 00:02:53.207
The direct collision between the two clusters occurred a few hundred million years ago,

00:02:53.647 --> 00:02:56.007
and the clusters are now moving away from each other.

00:02:56.968 --> 00:03:02.628
Imagine two city-sized clouds of hot gas, each containing hundreds of galaxies, sliding past

00:03:02.628 --> 00:03:04.888
each other at hundreds of kilometers per second.

00:03:05.488 --> 00:03:08.468
The distance between them would take light a million years to cross.

00:03:08.468 --> 00:03:13.748
That is the scale of the collision now reshaping the radio sky inside a distant cluster.

00:03:14.188 --> 00:03:18.848
The event compresses and re-energizes particles that have already traveled near light speed

00:03:18.848 --> 00:03:23.828
for millions of years, turning a faded radio glow back into a structure that looks, from

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:26.728
our vantage point, like a giant musical clef.

00:03:27.048 --> 00:03:31.168
The process begins with a supermassive black hole at the center of one cluster.

00:03:31.728 --> 00:03:37.128
Its earlier outburst flings clouds of charged particles and magnetic fields outward at relativistic

00:03:37.128 --> 00:03:37.568
speeds.

00:03:37.568 --> 00:03:43.028
seeds. Those particles radiate brightly for a time, then lose energy and fade. When

00:03:43.028 --> 00:03:47.368
the second cluster arrives, the resulting shockwaves and compression act like a cosmic

00:03:47.368 --> 00:03:53.568
bellows, squeezing the old particle population and boosting its radio output once more. Chandra

00:03:53.568 --> 00:03:59.408
and E. Rosita data show the hot gas is no longer symmetrical. The brightest x-ray residuals lie

00:03:59.408 --> 00:04:04.788
along the left right axis of the collision, confirming the direction of impact. What remains

00:04:04.788 --> 00:04:10.988
Unknown is why only some merging clusters produce such clean, recognizable radio shapes, while others do not.

00:04:11.588 --> 00:04:16.808
The same black hole plus collision recipe should operate elsewhere, yet this structure stands out.

00:04:17.388 --> 00:04:24.768
Differences in magnetic field strength, the exact timing between the outburst and the merger, or the viewing angle from Earth could all matter.

00:04:25.468 --> 00:04:33.668
Astronomers are now searching for more examples at similar red shifts to test which variable decides whether a faded radio cloud sings again or stays silent.

00:04:33.668 --> 00:04:39.170
Limb Darkening has now been observed on several nearby stars for the first time, according

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:40.090
to Universe Today.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.370
The edge of the sun appears darker than its central region.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.790
That effect is known as Limb Darkening and tells us about the solar atmosphere.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:52.530
This same Limb Darkening effect has now been observed on several nearby stars.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.450
The measurements could help improve understanding of stellar atmospheres.

00:04:57.438 --> 00:05:03.378
Chinese and Japanese sky watchers recorded a supernova that exploded in 1181, Universe

00:05:03.378 --> 00:05:08.518
today reports. They could not have known what the object was, or why it appeared for six months

00:05:08.518 --> 00:05:14.678
then disappeared. In modern times, we know it was a supernova, and we have even most likely found

00:05:14.678 --> 00:05:20.838
its remnant. New observations reveal more detail than ever in that remnant. The remnant is unusual

00:05:20.838 --> 00:05:27.087
and rare. NASA's Curiosity Rover recently captured a panorama from the slopes of Mount Sharp,

00:05:27.387 --> 00:05:32.407
according to Universe Today. The image shows a vista that was not white balance adjusted during

00:05:32.407 --> 00:05:37.707
processing. The processing choice was meant to show just what a viewer might really see at sunrise

00:05:37.707 --> 00:05:43.467
standing on Mars. A triplet of galaxies lacking dark matter is challenging theories, according to

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:49.267
Quanta magazine. A collision may have left these galaxies without dark matter. That finding spells

00:05:49.267 --> 00:05:55.549
trouble for alternative gravity theories. NASA's first missions to Venus in a generation are looking up

00:05:55.549 --> 00:06:01.769
again, ours' Technica reports. The missions had faced a cancellation threat. Things are finally looking

00:06:01.769 --> 00:06:06.829
up for those first NASA Venus missions in a generation. Rocket Labs' neutron rocket

00:06:06.829 --> 00:06:14.289
now looks likely to make its first flight in 2027, according to SciGuy Space. A 2027 debut

00:06:14.289 --> 00:06:20.469
is the probable timing. 2028 cannot be ruled out. A mission aims to Bruce Sake aboard

00:06:20.469 --> 00:06:26.609
the ISS, according to Spectra by MHI. The work sits inside an effort to Bruce Sake in space.

00:06:28.218 --> 00:06:31.758
CSIRO's space truck has marked its first year in the outback and beyond.

00:06:32.338 --> 00:06:33.418
Mirage news reports.

00:06:34.098 --> 00:06:37.958
The mobile exhibit has spent that year bringing space science to remote communities.

00:06:38.538 --> 00:06:40.978
The mark is a successful first year of operations.

00:06:41.618 --> 00:06:45.038
When astronauts first travel to Mars or anywhere else in deep space,

00:06:45.478 --> 00:06:48.198
they will have to contend with radiation and weightlessness,

00:06:48.638 --> 00:06:50.898
according to a study covered by Physa.org.

00:06:51.378 --> 00:06:53.778
Our planet protects us from both on its surface.

00:06:53.778 --> 00:06:59.538
scientists already know both can have a profound impact on the human body. The extent of that

00:06:59.538 --> 00:07:05.426
impact and how long it can last remain a mystery. Johnson Space Center's preview this week should

00:07:05.426 --> 00:07:11.266
lock in when Menon and Kutrick step outside to finish the last two rollout arrays. That's fascinating

